{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FRESH REVIEW: BLOCK, one finding, impact 1.0 — ACCEPTED, and I verified all three of its claims in\nsource before conceding.\n\nFIRST, THE GOOD NEWS ON THE EARLIER ODDITY: the stale findings are GONE. This run returned 0\nindeterminate, where the previous run reported findings quoting text that had already been removed.\nSo that was transient, and the decision NOT to re-edit in response to it was correct — re-editing\nwould have churned the description and invalidated the parent's pin for nothing. Leaving the note\nrather than acting on it was the right call.\n\nTHE NEW FINDING IS A REAL DEFECT IN MY PLAN, not a nitpick. AC1 said the registry-membership test\nmust FAIL when the registration import at workflow_ops.py:45 is removed, and offered \"prove it bites\nby deleting the :45 import locally\". That cannot work in a normal pytest run:\n  * STEP_REGISTRY is a process-global dict — `STEP_REGISTRY: dict[str, ScriptedStep] = {}` at\n    executor.py:155 — populated purely as an IMPORT SIDE EFFECT.\n  * Python caches modules in sys.modules, so the registry is populated ONCE per process.\n  * At least SIX test modules already import `rebar.llm.workflow.steps`, including\n    tests/unit/workflow/test_contracts.py:16 (`from rebar.llm.workflow import steps  # noqa: F401 -\n    registers the built-in contracts`), plus test_plan_review_triage.py,\n    test_code_review_deletion_impact.py, test_audit_code_review_v2.py, test_code_review_nit_routing.py\n    and test_code_review_project_criteria.py.\nSo by the time the new test runs, the registry is ALREADY full, and deleting the :45 import changes\nnothing. The guard would pass whether or not the defect it exists to catch is present.\n\nWHY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: this ticket's entire justification is that a missed registration\nimport is invisible to every existing layer (schema, linter, and test_contracts.py:186, which only\niterates what IS registered). Shipping a guard that is ITSELF unable to detect a missed registration\nwould reproduce the exact failure mode in the fix. A decorative guard is worse than none, because it\nreads as coverage.\n\nREMEDIATED: the membership assertion must run IN A SUBPROCESS with a clean sys.modules, using the\npattern the repo already has at tests/unit/test_structured_run_seam.py:115\n(`subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], ...)`) — the only form that observes a first-import\nregistry. A second AC now requires recording that the same check run IN-PROCESS stays GREEN under the\nsame deletion: that contrast is the evidence the subprocess form is necessary rather than merely\ntidier, and it stops a future reader from \"simplifying\" it back into the broken shape.\n\nThis is the second time on this ticket that the reviewer caught a claim I had asserted confidently\nwithout executing. Both times the fix was to test the claim rather than to argue it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785569451645222001,"uuid":"273f028b-b333-4faa-8696-715e63a3d49e"}